<section style="padding:0 24px;">
  <div style="max-width:860px;margin:0 auto 56px;">
    <h2 class="section-heading" style="font-size:clamp(22px,3vw,32px);margin:0 0 18px;">Rotating Equipment We Service and the Failure Modes We Chase</h2>
    <div class="rich"><p>Our rotating equipment services in Nigeria cover the machine classes that carry production: reciprocating and centrifugal compressors, boiler feed and transfer pumps, ID and FD fans, blowers, gearboxes and the motors or turbines that drive them. Each class fails in a recognisable way, so we set the inspection interval against the failure mode rather than against the calendar.</p><ul><li><strong>Reciprocating compressors:</strong> valve failure, ring and rider band wear, rod runout and packing leakage. Valve inspection every 4,000 running hours, top end overhaul around 8,000 hours, major overhaul between 24,000 and 32,000 hours.</li><li><strong>Centrifugal compressors:</strong> fouling, seal gas problems, rotor rub and surge damage. Monthly condition monitoring, major overhaul typically every 3 to 5 years or 25,000 to 40,000 running hours.</li><li><strong>Pumps:</strong> mechanical seal failure, wear ring clearance loss, cavitation erosion and bearing distress. Seal and bearing life of 18 to 36 months on clean duty, considerably less on abrasive or intermittent service.</li><li><strong>Fans and blowers:</strong> blade erosion and fouling that drives unbalance, bearing housing looseness and belt drive wear. Quarterly vibration checks, with balancing once velocity crosses the alarm zone.</li><li><strong>Drive trains and couplings:</strong> misalignment, coupling element fatigue and soft foot. Alignment verified after every rebuild and after any foundation, piping or baseplate work.</li></ul><p>Where the prime mover is a gas or steam turbine, the same crew handles the driver so the train is treated as one machine rather than two contracts. See <a href="/steam-turbine-overhaul-nigeria/">steam turbine overhaul</a> and <a href="/generator-maintenance-nigeria/">industrial generator maintenance</a> for that scope.</p></div>
  </div>
</section>
<section style="padding:0 24px;">
  <div style="max-width:860px;margin:0 auto 56px;">
    <h2 class="section-heading" style="font-size:clamp(22px,3vw,32px);margin:0 0 18px;">Vibration Analysis, Laser Alignment and Dynamic Balancing</h2>
    <div class="rich"><p>Diagnosis comes before disassembly. We collect vibration spectra, time waveform and phase at each bearing position under normal load, together with process readings such as suction and discharge pressure, flow, bearing temperature and motor current. That combined data set shows whether you have a mechanical defect, a process problem or a foundation problem, which are three different purchase orders.</p><p>Vibration severity is reported against ISO 20816 zones so the numbers mean the same thing to your team and ours. For most medium sized machines on rigid foundations we treat 2.8 mm/s rms as the point where a machine leaves good condition and 7.1 mm/s rms as the point where continued running threatens bearings and seals. Rolling element bearing defects appear in the envelope and high frequency bands well before overall velocity moves, and that early warning is what gives you time to order parts and plan the outage.</p><p>Alignment and balancing close out the job. We align shaft trains with a laser system to a residual offset target of 0.05 mm, correct soft foot first, and apply thermal growth offsets where the machine runs hot. Rotors are balanced in place to ISO 21940 grade G2.5 for general industrial duty, or G6.3 where the application and speed allow it.</p></div>
  </div>
</section>
<section style="padding:0 24px;">
  <div style="max-width:860px;margin:0 auto 56px;">
    <h2 class="section-heading" style="font-size:clamp(22px,3vw,32px);margin:0 0 18px;">Overhaul Scope: Seals, Couplings, Bearings and Control Panels</h2>
    <div class="rich"><p>A rebuild is only as good as the clearances and the parts that go back in. Our standard scope covers strip and clean, dimensional inspection of shafts, journals, wear rings and housings against OEM limits, and non destructive testing where cracking is a credible risk. We then replace bearings, mechanical seals, gaskets, packing and coupling elements, reassemble with recorded clearances and document the run in at rated load.</p><p>Every overhaul returns a document pack you can file against the asset and hand to the next crew:</p><ul><li>As found condition report with vibration, alignment and process data</li><li>Dimensional inspection sheet showing measured values against allowable clearances</li><li>Parts list with items replaced, part numbers and material specification</li><li>As left vibration and alignment readings taken at rated load</li><li>Recommended next inspection interval and the wear items to hold in stores</li></ul><p>Control and protection usually lag the mechanical work. We upgrade compressor and pump control panels, replace obsolete relays, contactors and starters, add vibration and temperature protection channels, and bring trip settings back in line with the machine's actual operating envelope. Where several machines share a panel or a common utility header, a <a href="/power-plant-audit-nigeria/">power plant audit</a> is the sensible starting point.</p></div>
  </div>
</section>
<section style="padding:0 24px;">
  <div style="max-width:860px;margin:0 auto 56px;">
    <h2 class="section-heading" style="font-size:clamp(22px,3vw,32px);margin:0 0 18px;">Reverse Engineering, Local Spares and How the Work Is Priced</h2>
    <div class="rich"><p>Import lead times and FX movement cost Nigerian plants more than the mechanical failures themselves. An impeller, shaft, sleeve or wear ring ordered from an OEM can take 10 to 20 weeks door to door before clearing, and the naira cost is fixed on the day of payment rather than the day of quotation. For obsolete machines the part may not be available at any price.</p><p>We capture the failed component dimensionally, verify material by hardness and composition checks, then manufacture the replacement locally in 3 to 6 weeks for most items. Impellers, shafts, sleeves, casing wear rings, gland followers and coupling spacers are routine work. Where the original design contributed to the failure, we say so and propose a material or clearance change instead of reproducing the fault.</p><p>For procurement, every proposal carries a fixed scope of work, a priced bill of materials, mobilisation and manpower separated from parts, the outage window in days and a clear list of exclusions. That is enough detail to raise a purchase order without a second round of clarifications. Indicative ranges sit on our <a href="/generator-turbine-maintenance-cost/">maintenance cost page</a>, and mobilisation is costed from the nearest base for <a href="/turbine-services-lagos/">Lagos</a>, <a href="/turbine-services-port-harcourt/">Port Harcourt</a>, <a href="/turbine-services-warri/">Warri</a>, <a href="/turbine-services-abuja/">Abuja</a>, <a href="/turbine-services-kaduna/">Kaduna</a> and <a href="/turbine-services-kano/">Kano</a>.</p></div>
  </div>
</section>
<section style="padding:72px 24px;background:#EAF5FB;">
  <div style="max-width:860px;margin:0 auto;">
    <span class="kicker">FAQ</span>
    <h2 class="section-heading" style="font-size:clamp(24px,3.5vw,36px);margin:0 0 32px;">Frequently Asked Questions</h2>
    <div style="display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:16px;">
      <div style="background:#fff;border:1px solid #e3ded4;border-radius:8px;padding:24px 28px;">
        <h3 style="font-family:'Nunito Sans',sans-serif;font-size:18px;font-weight:800;color:#0A3D5C;margin:0 0 10px;">How often should rotating equipment be checked with vibration analysis?</h3>
        <p style="font-size:15px;line-height:1.75;color:#555;margin:0;">For critical machines we recommend a monthly route, and quarterly as the minimum for balance of plant equipment. Reciprocating compressors still need valve inspection every 4,000 running hours regardless of what the vibration trend shows. A plant with 40 to 60 monitored points is normally covered in one to two days per visit, with the report issued within five working days.</p>
      </div>
      <div style="background:#fff;border:1px solid #e3ded4;border-radius:8px;padding:24px 28px;">
        <h3 style="font-family:'Nunito Sans',sans-serif;font-size:18px;font-weight:800;color:#0A3D5C;margin:0 0 10px;">What does a rotating equipment survey cost in Nigeria?</h3>
        <p style="font-size:15px;line-height:1.75;color:#555;margin:0;">A baseline vibration and alignment survey runs from about NGN 900,000 (roughly USD 600) for a small plant with 20 to 30 measurement points, up to NGN 2,800,000 (roughly USD 1,850) for a large site with 100 points and full reporting. Compressor and pump overhauls are quoted separately once the survey has defined the scope, so you are not paying for parts nobody has proved you need. Mobilisation outside Lagos is priced per trip and shown as a separate line.</p>
      </div>
      <div style="background:#fff;border:1px solid #e3ded4;border-radius:8px;padding:24px 28px;">
        <h3 style="font-family:'Nunito Sans',sans-serif;font-size:18px;font-weight:800;color:#0A3D5C;margin:0 0 10px;">Can you manufacture spares for obsolete compressors and pumps?</h3>
        <p style="font-size:15px;line-height:1.75;color:#555;margin:0;">Yes. We reverse engineer impellers, shafts, sleeves, wear rings, coupling components and gland parts from the failed item or from a drawing, with material verified before machining begins. Typical delivery is 3 to 6 weeks against 10 to 20 weeks for an imported OEM part. Pricing is settled in naira, so FX movement during the lead time does not reopen the cost.</p>
      </div>
      <div style="background:#fff;border:1px solid #e3ded4;border-radius:8px;padding:24px 28px;">
        <h3 style="font-family:'Nunito Sans',sans-serif;font-size:18px;font-weight:800;color:#0A3D5C;margin:0 0 10px;">How long does a compressor or pump overhaul take?</h3>
        <p style="font-size:15px;line-height:1.75;color:#555;margin:0;">A site pump overhaul is usually 2 to 4 days once parts are on the ground. A reciprocating compressor top end overhaul takes 4 to 7 days, while a major overhaul involving crankshaft and frame work runs 2 to 4 weeks. Centrifugal compressor majors are planned around your shutdown window and normally need 3 to 6 weeks including rotor work and balancing.</p>
      </div>
      <div style="background:#fff;border:1px solid #e3ded4;border-radius:8px;padding:24px 28px;">
        <h3 style="font-family:'Nunito Sans',sans-serif;font-size:18px;font-weight:800;color:#0A3D5C;margin:0 0 10px;">Do you cover plants outside Lagos?</h3>
        <p style="font-size:15px;line-height:1.75;color:#555;margin:0;">Yes. We mobilise from Lagos to Rivers, FCT, Kaduna, Delta and Kano States, with planned work typically starting within 5 to 10 working days of a purchase order. Emergency mobilisation for a stopped critical machine is arranged case by case depending on crew and equipment availability. To book a plant assessment or request a technical proposal, call +234 803 000 0000 or email info@axiompower.com.ng.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>
